--pos 1st competition--
#day 1#
  After observing flaws in the competition and experiencing the dynamics firsthand, it was decided to implement corrections and modularize the code to achieve a more reliable and secure strategy. 
  One of the initial issues identified was a "blind spot" when starting too close to the front wall; because the robot was positioned so close to the inner wall, 
  its field of view and the logic used to determine direction caused it to consistently choose the wrong path—specifically, the opposite of the correct one. 
  To correct this the original code was first split into two separate files—separating Mode 1 from Mode 2—resulting in better performance and a more manageable workflow. 
  To fix errors identified in Mode 1, the structure was overhauled to minimize complexity, streamlining the code as much as possible while ensuring reliability. 
  The PID controller and its associated components were removed in favor of a Bang-Bang (or on/off) control methodology, which had proven most effective for our robot during the previous competition; 
  this approach allowed us to complete the first challenge more consistently and with a more easily adjustable configuration. For Mode 2, we are implementing Dead Reckoning navigation, 
  which offers far greater consistency than what we achieved during the last competition; however, given that this method is susceptible to drift or timing offsets, 
  its final implementation is being evaluated ahead of the upcoming competition.

#day 2#
  We didn't find any major flaws in the code used so far. New commands were added to the code for the second mode to create a code base that can then be modified as needed on the day of the competition, 
  The car has responded correctly in most of the tests.

